INT. SPEAKEASY BEHIND A FUNERAL PARLOR - NIGHT, 1923

Caskets stacked as bar-backs. A gramophone croons. Candlelight.

MAMIE DUPREE, 30s, bartender, silver flapper dress, polishes a glass. Everything is lovely and slow.

A MAN IN A GREY HAT, 40s, sits at the bar. He turns his drink in circles. Doesn't drink.

BARNABY SCOTT, 60s, the undertaker, dozes on a coffin lid in the corner.

MAMIE
You're not from here.

MAN IN GREY HAT
No.

MAMIE
You like the music?

MAN IN GREY HAT
It's fine.

MAMIE
It's a funeral parlor. We play what the dead like.

He almost smiles. Takes a sip. Sets the glass down.

MAN IN GREY HAT (CONT'D)
Your license?

Mamie's polishing stops. Barnaby's snore stops.

MAMIE
Pardon?

The man pulls back his coat. A badge.

ACKERLY
Solomon Ackerly. Prohibition Bureau.

Mamie lowers the glass. Slowly. Barnaby sits up in his coffin.

BARNABY
Mamie. The other room.

MAMIE
(calm)
Detective, we're only mourning.

ACKERLY
In evening dress.

She glances at the gramophone. Reaches. Turns the volume UP.

The gramophone swells. Behind the bar, the wall begins to slide open.
